{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22343","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22343","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22343","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garrett","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Garrett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Weiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiayuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie J.","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Weijie J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T15:53:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:44:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-30T15:33:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:11:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text watermarks in large language models (LLMs) are an increasingly important tool for detecting synthetic text and distinguishing human-written content from LLM-generated text. While most existing studies focus on determining whether entire texts are watermarked, many real-world scenarios involve mixed-source texts, which blend human-written and watermarked content. In this paper, we address the problem of optimally estimating the watermark proportion in mixed-source texts. We cast this problem as estimating the proportion parameter in a mixture model based on \\emph{pivotal statistics}. First, we show that this parameter is not even identifiable in certain watermarking schemes, let alone consistently estimable. In stark contrast, for watermarking methods that employ continuous pivotal statistics for detection, we demonstrate that the proportion parameter is identifiable under mild conditions. We propose efficient estimators for this class of methods, which include several popular unbiased watermarks as examples, and derive minimax lower bounds for any measurable estimator based on pivotal statistics, showing that our estimators achieve these lower bounds. Through evaluations on both synthetic data and mixed-source text generated by open-source models, we demonstrate that our proposed estimators consistently achieve high estimation accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22343","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:05Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T04:59:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22316","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22316","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22316","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingquan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qingquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyu","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Shaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kailai","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Kailai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Haixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Scoring Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T15:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:43:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-25T07:37:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-26T01:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-26T02:31:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08-27T00:17:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-03T11:44:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:53:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-981-92-0372-7_2","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The \"LLM-as-a-Judge\" paradigm, using Large Language Models (LLMs) as automated evaluators, is pivotal to LLM development, offering scalable feedback for complex tasks. However, the reliability of these judges is compromised by various biases. Existing research has heavily concentrated on biases in comparative evaluations. In contrast, scoring-based evaluations-which assign an absolute score and are often more practical in industrial applications-remain under-investigated. To address this gap, we undertake the first dedicated examination of scoring bias in LLM judges. We shift the focus from biases tied to the evaluation targets to those originating from the scoring prompt itself. We formally define scoring bias and identify three novel, previously unstudied types: rubric order bias, score ID bias, and reference answer score bias. We propose a comprehensive framework to quantify these biases, featuring a suite of multi-faceted metrics and an automatic data synthesis pipeline to create a tailored evaluation corpus. Our experiments empirically demonstrate that even the most advanced LLMs suffer from these substantial scoring biases. Our analysis yields actionable insights for designing more robust scoring prompts and mitigating these newly identified biases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by DASFAA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22316","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-06-30T01:39:26Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:39:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:28:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunke","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yunke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Visual Token Reduction in LVLMs Under Cross-Modal Misalignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:55:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:41:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-03T11:00:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-05T00:48:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-28T04:38:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:26:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) encode visual inputs as dense sequences of patch-level tokens to capture fine-grained semantics. These visual tokens often outnumber their textual counterparts by a large margin, leading to substantial computational overhead and limiting the scalability of LVLMs in practice. Previous efforts have explored visual token reduction either prior to or within the large language models (LLMs). However, most in-LLM reduction approaches rely on text-conditioned interactions, implicitly assuming that textual tokens can reliably capture the importance of visual tokens. In this work, we revisit this assumption and reveal causal, semantic, and spatial forms of cross-modal misalignment. These misalignments undermine the effectiveness of text-guided visual token reduction. To address this, we introduce VisionDrop, a training-free, visual-only pruning framework that selects informative visual tokens based on intra-modal (visual-to-visual) attention, without relying on textual signals. To further suppress redundancy throughout the model hierarchy, we treat the visual encoder and the LLM as a unified system and design a progressive pruning pipeline. Our method performs dominant token selection and lightweight contextual merging at multiple stages, enabling fine-grained visual information to be retained even under aggressive token budgets. Extensive experiments across diverse benchmarks show that VisionDrop achieves consistent improvements over existing approaches, despite requiring no additional training or complex modifications. Notably, when integrated with LLaVA-NeXT-7B, VisionDrop achieves a 2.7x reduction in inference latency and 6x in FLOPs, while retaining 95.71% of the original performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"AAAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:38:39Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:38:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:39:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22376","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22376","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22376","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youkang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Youkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rubing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Rubing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Yong","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xiao-Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OptScale: Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:44:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:47:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-10T16:42:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-12T01:00:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T17:04:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-19T02:00:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-19T10:17:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:32:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inference-time scaling has emerged as a powerful technique for enhancing the reasoning performance of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, existing approaches often rely on heuristic strategies for parallel sampling, lacking a principled foundation. To address this gap, we propose a probabilistic framework that formalizes the optimality of inference-time scaling under the assumption that parallel samples are independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.), and where the Best-of-$N$ selection strategy follows a probability distribution that can be estimated. Within this framework, we derive a theoretical lower bound on the required number of samples to achieve a target performance level, providing the first principled guidance for compute-efficient scaling. Leveraging this insight, we develop \\textsc{OptScale}, a practical algorithm that dynamically determines the optimal number of sampled responses. \\textsc{OptScale} employs a language model-based predictor to estimate probabilistic prior parameters, enabling the decision of the minimal number of samples needed that satisfy predefined performance thresholds and confidence levels. Extensive experiments on representative reasoning benchmarks (including MATH-500, GSM8K, AIME, and AMC) demonstrate that \\textsc{OptScale} significantly reduces sampling overhead while remaining better or on par with state-of-the-art reasoning performance. Our work offers both a theoretical foundation and a practical solution for principled inference-time scaling, addressing a critical gap in the efficient deployment of LLMs for complex reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by AAAI-2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22376","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:51Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-22T03:01:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22200","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22200","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22200","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Lai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zedong","familyName":"Dan","name":"Dan, Zedong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EFRame: Deeper Reasoning via Exploration-Filter-Replay Reinforcement Learning Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T13:09:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:37:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T07:33:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-01T01:04:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-07T11:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-08T01:49:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-01T05:28:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:30:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:14:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in reinforcement learning (RL) have significantly enhanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), a lightweight variant of Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), improves efficiency but suffers from limited exploration and training instability, limiting its effectiveness on complex reasoning tasks. To address these challenges, we introduce EFRame, an Exploration-Filter-Replay framework that augments GRPO across three dimensions: additional rollouts enable deeper and more targeted exploration, online filtering removes low-quality samples to stabilize gradients and accelerate training, and experience replay amplifies rare yet informative trajectories for stable convergence. This unified framework establishes a principled training cycle that balances exploration, efficiency, and stability. Experiments on diverse reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that EFRame achieves consistent gains, including a 37.9\\% relative improvement on Geometry3K over GRPO. EFRame further supports fine-grained sample categorization and precise entropy control, highlighting it as a robust solution for advancing deeper reasoning in LLMs. Our code is available at https://github.com/597358816/EFRame."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22200","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:36:41Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:36:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:13:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22385","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22385","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22385","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jilei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jilei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhui","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yunhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhav","familyName":"Gogate","name":"Gogate, Vibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Video Large Multimodal Models Think Like Doubters-or Double-Down: A Study on Defeasible Video Entailment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:51:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:47:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T03:47:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:22:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Large Multimodal Models (VLMMs) have made impressive strides in understanding video content, but they often struggle with abstract and adaptive reasoning-the ability to revise their interpretations when new information emerges. In reality, conclusions are rarely set in stone; additional context can strengthen or weaken an initial inference. To address this, we introduce Defeasible Video Entailment (DVidE), a new task that challenges models to think like doubters, constantly updating their reasoning based on evolving evidence. In DVidE, given a video premise and a textual hypothesis, models must determine whether a new update strengthens or weakens the hypothesis (classification version) or generate a coherent update that modifies the entailment relationship (generation version). For solving the classification task, we propose the Chain of Counterfactual Thought framework, utilizing counterfactual reasoning, ASR-enhanced video content, and rationale refinement to reduce inference bias. For the generation task, we develop a framework that combines ASR output with a Large Language Model (LLM) to produce coherent, contextually relevant updates aligned with the intended strengthener or weakener goals. Additionally, we introduce a novel benchmark dataset, with strengthener/weakener annotations and an LLM-based evaluation metric specifically designed for assessing generative performance. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements, highlighting our proposed method in enhancing dynamic reasoning capabilities of VLMMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22385","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:04Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:23:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.01052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2507.01052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Farooq","name":"Farooq, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Long-Sequence Memory with Temporal Kernels and Dense Hopfield Functionals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T15:57:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-03T00:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this study we introduce a novel energy functional for long-sequence memory, building upon the framework of dense Hopfield networks which achieves exponential storage capacity through higher-order interactions. Building upon earlier work on long-sequence Hopfield memory models, we propose a temporal kernal $K(m, k)$ to incorporate temporal dependencies, enabling efficient sequential retrieval of patterns over extended sequences. We demonstrate the successful application of this technique for the storage and sequential retrieval of movies frames which are well suited for this because of the high dimensional vectors that make up each frame creating enough variation between even sequential frames in the high dimensional space. The technique has applications in modern transformer architectures, including efficient long-sequence modeling, memory augmentation, improved attention with temporal bias, and enhanced handling of long-term dependencies in time-series data. Our model offers a promising approach to address the limitations of transformers in long-context tasks, with potential implications for natural language processing, forecasting, and beyond."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.01052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-07-03T01:30:38Z","registered":"2025-07-03T01:30:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-03T01:30:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22403","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22403","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22403","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"NAVER Cloud HyperCLOVA X Team","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HyperCLOVA X THINK Technical Report"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:23:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:48:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-01T13:39:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-02T00:41:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce HyperCLOVA X THINK, the first reasoning-focused large language model in the HyperCLOVA X family, pre-trained on roughly $6$ trillion high-quality Korean, and English tokens, augmented with targeted synthetic Korean data. It was implemented as a compute-memory-balanced Peri-LN Transformer scaled with $μ$P, pre-trained through a three-stage curriculum that expands the context window to $128$K tokens, and post-trained via supervised fine-tuning with Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards supports both detailed rationale and concise-answer modes. It delivers competitive performance against similarly sized models on Korea-focused benchmarks such as KMMLU, CSAT, KoBALT-700, HAERAE-1.0, and KoBigBench, while preserving robust bilingual consistency and translation quality. In addition, a vision-augmented variant matches or exceeds GPT-4.1 on the KCSAT STEM benchmark, all of which are achieved with substantially lower training compute than existing models of similar sizes. We also present a pruning and distillation technique that will soon be applied to HyperCLOVA X THINK for an open-source and business-friendly foundation model. Altogether, these capabilities position HyperCLOVA X THINK as a robust foundation for Korean AI innovation and a valuable resource for the global research community."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"50 pages, 13 figures; fixed figures in the appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22403","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:29Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-02T02:05:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Mingkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoteng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shaoteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaogang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaogang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengshuang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hengshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MiCo: Multi-image Contrast for Reinforcement Visual Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:59:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:50:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work explores enabling Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to link visual cues across multiple images. A straightforward solution is to adapt rule-based reinforcement learning for Vision-Language Models (VLMs). However, such methods typically rely on manually curated question-answer pairs, which can be particularly challenging when dealing with fine grained visual details and complex logic across images. Inspired by self-supervised visual representation learning, we observe that images contain inherent constraints that can serve as supervision. Based on this insight, we construct image triplets comprising two augmented views of the same image and a third, similar but distinct image. During training, the model is prompted to generate a reasoning process to compare these images (i.e., determine same or different). Then we optimize the model with rule-based reinforcement learning. Due to the high visual similarity and the presence of augmentations, the model must attend to subtle visual changes and perform logical reasoning to succeed. Experiments show that, although trained solely on visual comparison tasks, the learned reasoning ability generalizes effectively to a wide range of questions. Without relying on any human-annotated question-answer pairs, our method achieves significant improvements on multi-image reasoning benchmarks and shows strong performance on general vision tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:42:13Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:42:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:42:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuejie","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Yuejie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploration from a Primal-Dual Lens: Value-Incentivized Actor-Critic Methods for Sample-Efficient Online RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:18:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online reinforcement learning (RL) with complex function approximations such as transformers and deep neural networks plays a significant role in the modern practice of artificial intelligence. Despite its popularity and importance, balancing the fundamental trade-off between exploration and exploitation remains a long-standing challenge; in particular, we are still in lack of efficient and practical schemes that are backed by theoretical performance guarantees. Motivated by recent developments in exploration via optimistic regularization, this paper provides an interpretation of the principle of optimism through the lens of primal-dual optimization. From this fresh perspective, we set forth a new value-incentivized actor-critic (VAC) method, which optimizes a single easy-to-optimize objective integrating exploration and exploitation -- it promotes state-action and policy estimates that are both consistent with collected data transitions and result in higher value functions. Theoretically, the proposed VAC method has near-optimal regret guarantees under linear Markov decision processes (MDPs) in both finite-horizon and infinite-horizon settings, which can be extended to the general function approximation setting under appropriate assumptions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:27Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:41:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danush","familyName":"Khanna","name":"Khanna, Danush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya Kumar","familyName":"Guru","name":"Guru, Aditya Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srivarshinee","familyName":"Sridhar","name":"Sridhar, Srivarshinee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zidan","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Zidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rubhav","familyName":"Bahirwani","name":"Bahirwani, Rubhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meetu","familyName":"Malhotra","name":"Malhotra, Meetu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinija","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Vinija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Chadha","name":"Chadha, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amitava","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Amitava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kripabandhu","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Kripabandhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuickSilver -- Speeding up LLM Inference through Dynamic Token Halting, KV Skipping, Contextual Token Fusion, and Adaptive Matryoshka Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.0; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:10:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:48:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inference accounts for the majority of latency and energy consumption in large language model (LLM) deployments, often exceeding 90% of total cost. While training-time efficiency has seen extensive progress, runtime optimization remains a key bottleneck, particularly under autoregressive decoding. Existing approaches -- such as pruning, quantization, early exits, and speculative decoding -- often require retraining, architectural changes, or disrupt decoding compatibility. We introduce QuickSilver, a modular, token-level framework that enables semantic adaptivity at inference time without altering model weights or structure. QuickSilver integrates four synergistic mechanisms:\n (i) Dynamic Token Halting, which halts computation for tokens with converged representations; (ii) KV Cache Skipping, which selectively suppresses memory writes to reduce attention overhead; and (iii) Contextual Token Fusion, which collapses redundant tokens into shared paths to shrink sequence length.\n Unlike speculative decoding or MoE routing, QuickSilver operates entirely on frozen, dense models and requires no auxiliary networks. Applied to GPT-2 and Llama-2 across WikiText-103 and C4, QuickSilver achieves up to 39.6% FLOP reduction with negligible perplexity degradation (\u0026lt;=0.2)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Under submission"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:20Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:41:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22395","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22395","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22395","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shih-Han","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Shih-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivam","familyName":"Chandhok","name":"Chandhok, Shivam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James J.","familyName":"Little","name":"Little, James J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonid","familyName":"Sigal","name":"Sigal, Leonid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Consistency in Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T17:09:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:48:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have achieved impressive performance across a wide range of multimodal tasks, yet they often exhibit inconsistent behavior when faced with semantically equivalent inputs, undermining their reliability and robustness. Recent benchmarks, such as MM-R3, highlight that even state-of-the-art VLMs can produce divergent predictions across semantically equivalent inputs, despite maintaining high average accuracy. Prior work addresses this issue by modifying model architectures or conducting large-scale fine-tuning on curated datasets. In contrast, we propose a simple and effective test-time consistency framework that enhances semantic consistency without supervised re-training. Our method is entirely post-hoc, model-agnostic, and applicable to any VLM with access to its weights. Given a single test point, we enforce consistent predictions via two complementary objectives: (i) a Cross-Entropy Agreement Loss that aligns predictive distributions across semantically equivalent inputs, and (ii) a Pseudo-Label Consistency Loss that draws outputs toward a self-averaged consensus. Our method is plug-and-play and leverages information from a single test input itself to improve consistency. Experiments on the MM-R3 benchmark show that our framework yields substantial gains in consistency across state-of-the-art models, establishing a new direction for inference-time adaptation in multimodal learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22395","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:18Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:41:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22389","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22389","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22389","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Cowsik","name":"Cowsik, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrey","familyName":"Gromov","name":"Gromov, Andrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Distributed Neural Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:47:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce and train distributed neural architectures (DNA) in vision and language domains. DNAs are initialized with a proto-architecture that consists of (transformer, MLP, attention, etc.) modules and routers. Any token (or patch) can traverse any series of modules in any order. DNAs are a natural generalization of the sparse methods such as Mixture-of-Experts, Mixture-of-Depths, parameter sharing, etc. Computation and communication patterns of DNA modules are learnt end-to-end during training and depend on the content and context of each token (or patch). These patterns can be shaped by further requirements added to the optimization objective such as compute/memory efficiency or load balancing. We empirically show that (i) trained DNAs are competitive with the dense baselines in both domains and (ii) compute efficiency/parameter sharing can be learnt from data. Next, we analyze the emergent connectivity and computation patterns in the trained DNAs. We find that the paths that tokens take through the models are themselves distributed according to a power-law. We show that some paths (or, equivalently, groups of modules) show emergent specialization. Finally, we demonstrate that models learn to allocate compute and active parameters in an interpretable way."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, 25 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22389","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:41:10Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:41:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:41:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22375","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22375","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22375","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiankai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tiankai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Goodge","name":"Goodge, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xulei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xulei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploiting Vision Language Model for Training-Free 3D Point Cloud OOD Detection via Graph Score Propagation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:42:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:47:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection in 3D point cloud data remains a challenge, particularly in applications where safe and robust perception is critical. While existing OOD detection methods have shown progress for 2D image data, extending these to 3D environments involves unique obstacles. This paper introduces a training-free framework that leverages Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for effective OOD detection in 3D point clouds. By constructing a graph based on class prototypes and testing data, we exploit the data manifold structure to enhancing the effectiveness of VLMs for 3D OOD detection. We propose a novel Graph Score Propagation (GSP) method that incorporates prompt clustering and self-training negative prompting to improve OOD scoring with VLM. Our method is also adaptable to few-shot scenarios, providing options for practical applications. We demonstrate that GSP consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods across synthetic and real-world datasets 3D point cloud OOD detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22375","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:50Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:40:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Sung","name":"Sung, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shao-Fu","familyName":"Shih","name":"Shih, Shao-Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Craig","familyName":"Dooley","name":"Dooley, Craig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Centazzo","name":"Centazzo, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramanan","familyName":"Rajeswaran","name":"Rajeswaran, Ramanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffSoundStream: Efficient Speech Tokenization via Diffusion Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T16:23:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:46:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Token-based language modeling is a prominent approach for speech generation, where tokens are obtained by quantizing features from self-supervised learning (SSL) models and extracting codes from neural speech codecs, generally referred to as semantic tokens and acoustic tokens. These tokens are often modeled autoregressively, with the inference speed being constrained by the token rate. In this work, we propose DiffSoundStream, a solution that improves the efficiency of speech tokenization in non-streaming scenarios through two techniques: (1) conditioning the neural codec on semantic tokens to minimize redundancy between semantic and acoustic tokens, and (2) leveraging latent diffusion models to synthesize high-quality waveforms from semantic and coarse-level acoustic tokens. Experiments show that at 50 tokens per second, DiffSoundStream achieves speech quality on par with a standard SoundStream model operating at twice the token rate. Additionally, we achieve step-size distillation using just four diffusion sampling steps with only a minor quality loss."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:40:32Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:40:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:40:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22305","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22305","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22305","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert Agisha","familyName":"Ntwali","name":"Ntwali, Albert Agisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Rück","name":"Rück, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Heckmann","name":"Heckmann, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detection of Personal Data in Structured Datasets Using a Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.5.4; I.2.7; H.3.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T15:16:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:42:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a novel approach for detecting personal data in structured datasets, leveraging GPT-4o, a state-of-the-art Large Language Model. A key innovation of our method is the incorporation of contextual information: in addition to a feature's name and values, we utilize information from other feature names within the dataset as well as the dataset description. We compare our approach to alternative methods, including Microsoft Presidio and CASSED, evaluating them on multiple datasets: DeSSI, a large synthetic dataset, datasets we collected from Kaggle and OpenML as well as MIMIC-Demo-Ext, a real-world dataset containing patient information from critical care units.\n Our findings reveal that detection performance varies significantly depending on the dataset used for evaluation. CASSED excels on DeSSI, the dataset on which it was trained. Performance on the medical dataset MIMIC-Demo-Ext is comparable across all models, with our GPT-4o-based approach clearly outperforming the others. Notably, personal data detection in the Kaggle and OpenML datasets appears to benefit from contextual information. This is evidenced by the poor performance of CASSED and Presidio (both of which do not utilize the context of the dataset) compared to the strong results of our GPT-4o-based approach.\n We conclude that further progress in this field would greatly benefit from the availability of more real-world datasets containing personal information."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22305","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:39:10Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:39:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:39:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22298","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22298","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22298","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianzhuang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jianzhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjing","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Renjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fenglong","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Fenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OutDreamer: Video Outpainting with a Diffusion Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T15:08:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:42:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video outpainting is a challenging task that generates new video content by extending beyond the boundaries of an original input video, requiring both temporal and spatial consistency. Many state-of-the-art methods utilize latent diffusion models with U-Net backbones but still struggle to achieve high quality and adaptability in generated content. Diffusion transformers (DiTs) have emerged as a promising alternative because of their superior performance. We introduce OutDreamer, a DiT-based video outpainting framework comprising two main components: an efficient video control branch and a conditional outpainting branch. The efficient video control branch effectively extracts masked video information, while the conditional outpainting branch generates missing content based on these extracted conditions. Additionally, we propose a mask-driven self-attention layer that dynamically integrates the given mask information, further enhancing the model's adaptability to outpainting tasks. Furthermore, we introduce a latent alignment loss to maintain overall consistency both within and between frames. For long video outpainting, we employ a cross-video-clip refiner to iteratively generate missing content, ensuring temporal consistency across video clips. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that our zero-shot OutDreamer outperforms state-of-the-art zero-shot methods on widely recognized benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22298","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:39:00Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:39:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:39:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Stefaniak","name":"Stefaniak, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Krutul","name":"Krutul, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Małaśnicki","name":"Małaśnicki, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Pióro","name":"Pióro, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Krajewski","name":"Krajewski, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Jaszczur","name":"Jaszczur, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marek","familyName":"Cygan","name":"Cygan, Marek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamil","familyName":"Adamczewski","name":"Adamczewski, Kamil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Ludziejewski","name":"Ludziejewski, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Projected Compression: Trainable Projection for Efficient Transformer Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T14:24:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:40:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have steadily increased in size to achieve improved performance; however, this growth has also led to greater inference time and computational demands. Consequently, there is rising interest in model size reduction methods. To address this issue, we propose Projected Compression, a novel model compression technique, that reduces model weights by utilizing projection modules. Specifically, we first train additional trainable projections weights and preserve access to all the original model parameters. Subsequently, these projections are merged into a lower-dimensional product matrix, resulting in a reduced-size standard Transformer-based model. Unlike alternative approaches that require additional computational overhead, our method matches the base model's per-token computation step in FLOPs. Experimental results show that Projected Compression outperforms the comparable hard pruning and retraining approach on higher quality models. Moreover, the performance margin scales well with the number of tokens."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:37:59Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:37:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:37:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqi","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dazhao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Dazhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MPipeMoE: Memory Efficient MoE for Pre-trained Models with Adaptive Pipeline Parallelism"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T12:41:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ipdps54959.2023.00026","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) has become one of the most popular techniques to scale pre-trained models to extraordinarily large sizes. Dynamic activation of experts allows for conditional computation, increasing the number of parameters of neural networks, which is critical for absorbing the vast amounts of knowledge available in many deep learning areas. However, despite the existing system and algorithm optimizations, there are significant challenges to be tackled when it comes to the inefficiencies of communication and memory consumption.\n In this paper, we present the design and implementation of MPipeMoE, a high-performance library that accelerates MoE training with adaptive and memory-efficient pipeline parallelism. Inspired by that the MoE training procedure can be divided into multiple independent sub-stages, we design adaptive pipeline parallelism with an online algorithm to configure the granularity of the pipelining. Further, we analyze the memory footprint breakdown of MoE training and identify that activations and temporary buffers are the primary contributors to the overall memory footprint. Toward memory efficiency, we propose memory reusing strategies to reduce memory requirements by eliminating memory redundancies, and develop an adaptive selection component to determine the optimal strategy that considers both hardware capacities and model characteristics at runtime. We implement MPipeMoE upon PyTorch and evaluate it with common MoE models in a physical cluster consisting of 8 NVIDIA DGX A100 servers. Compared with the state-of-art approach, MPipeMoE achieves up to 2.8x speedup and reduces memory footprint by up to 47% in training large models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, accepted at IPDPS 2023"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-06-30T01:36:06Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:36:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:36:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22157","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22157","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22157","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tianshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingchuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mingchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bosi","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Bosi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cunxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuxiong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chuxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minlie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Minlie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T12:10:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:34:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable evaluation and critique capabilities, providing insightful feedback and identifying flaws in various tasks. However, limited research has explored which types of critiques are most effective for improving model responses or how to generate such critiques. To address this gap, we introduce \\textbf{R}efinement-oriented \\textbf{C}ritique \\textbf{O}ptimization (RCO), a novel framework designed to train critic models using refinement signals. RCO uses a feedback loop where critiques, generated by the critic model, guide the actor model in refining its responses. The critique utility (CU) quantifies the effectiveness of these refinements, serving as the reward signal for training the critic model. By focusing on critiques that lead to better refinements, RCO eliminates the need for direct critique preference assessment, ensuring that critiques driving meaningful improvements are rewarded. We evaluate RCO across five tasks, i.e., dialog generation, summarization, question answering, mathematical reasoning, and code generation, and show that it significantly outperforms traditional methods and open-source models in terms of critique quality and refinement outcomes. Our contributions include the introduction of RCO, a novel supervision scheme based on refined response preferences, and comprehensive experimental results that highlight the method's effectiveness in enhancing LLM critique-refinement loops."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22157","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:35:40Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:35:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:35:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":20,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%28doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22157%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22175%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22200%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22255%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22283%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22298%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22305%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22316%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22343%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22362%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22375%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22376%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22385%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22389%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22395%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22396%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22401%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22403%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22434%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2507.01052%22%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}